Looking at this problem, I need to find $f(m)$, the minimum number of briefing sessions so that every agent listens to every other agent at least once.

## Formalization

Each session partitions agents into **presenters** ($P$) and **listeners** ($L$). For every ordered pair $(i, j)$, $i \neq j$, we need a session where $j \in P$ and $i \in L$ (agent $i$ listens to agent $j$).

After $k$ sessions, each agent gets a **signature** in $\{0,1\}^k$ (1 = presenter, 0 = listener in that session). For each unordered pair $\{i, j\}$, we need:
- A coordinate where $s(i) = 0, s(j) = 1$ (i listens to j),
- A coordinate where $s(i) = 1, s(j) = 0$ (j listens to i).

This means neither signature is component-wise $\leq$ the other — the signatures form an **antichain** in the Boolean lattice $\{0,1\}^k$.

## Applying Sperner's Theorem

By **Sperner's Theorem**, the maximum antichain in $\{0,1\}^k$ has size $\binom{k}{\lfloor k/2 \rfloor}$, achieved by all vectors of weight $\lfloor k/2 \rfloor$.

Therefore:
$$f(m) = \min\left\{k : \binom{k}{\lfloor k/2 \rfloor} \geq m\right\}$$

## Computing the Central Binomial Coefficients

| $k$ | $\binom{k}{\lfloor k/2\rfloor}$ |
|-----|------|
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 |
| 2 | 2 |
| 3 | 3 |
| 4 | 6 |
| 5 | 10 |
| 6 | 20 |

## Determining $f(m)$ for $m = 1$ to $20$

- $f(1) = 0$ (no pairs, vacuously satisfied; $\binom{0}{0}=1 \geq 1$)
- $f(2) = 2$ ($\binom{1}{0}=1 < 2$; $\binom{2}{1}=2 \geq 2$)
- $f(3) = 3$ ($\binom{2}{1}=2 < 3$; $\binom{3}{1}=3 \geq 3$)
- $f(4) = f(5) = f(6) = 4$ ($\binom{3}{1}=3 < 4$; $\binom{4}{2}=6 \geq 4,5,6$)
- $f(7) = f(8) = f(9) = f(10) = 5$ ($\binom{4}{2}=6 < 7$; $\binom{5}{2}=10 \geq 7,\ldots,10$)
- $f(11) = \cdots = f(20) = 6$ ($\binom{5}{2}=10 < 11$; $\binom{6}{3}=20 \geq 11,\ldots,20$)

## Verification of the Construction

For $k$ sessions, assign each of the $m$ agents a distinct subset of $[k]$ of size $\lfloor k/2 \rfloor$. Since all signatures have the same weight, no one is component-wise $\leq$ another, so the antichain condition holds. For any pair $\{i,j\}$, since their subsets differ, there exists an element in $i$'s set not in $j$'s (giving $s(i)=1, s(j)=0$) and vice versa (giving $s(i)=0, s(j)=1$). Both directions are covered. ✓

## Computing the Sum

$$\sum_{m=1}^{20} f(m) = \underbrace{0}_{m=1} + \underbrace{2}_{m=2} + \underbrace{3}_{m=3} + \underbrace{4 \cdot 3}_{m=4,5,6} + \underbrace{5 \cdot 4}_{m=7,8,9,10} + \underbrace{6 \cdot 10}_{m=11,\ldots,20}$$

$$= 0 + 2 + 3 + 12 + 20 + 60 = \boxed{97}$$

